Håvard. Do you have any suggestions for what I could try to ensure I have the intended effect with my prior here (i.e. large positive prior mean should increase trt estimate)?

On Friday, December 22, 2023 at 8:25:10 a.m. UTC-5 Helpdesk (Haavard Rue) wrote:
>
> looks like you got it right. changing the mean with a 'large variance'
> is less influencial than increasing the precision.
>
> On Tue, 2023-12-19 at 13:36 -0800, Arlene Jiang wrote:
> > Hi,
> >
> > I am looking to set a prior on covariate in a proportional hazards
> > model. Based on the following examples, I am not sure I understand how
> > the prior influences the covariate effect estimate. I adapted some
> > code from the Bayesian inference with INLA book
> > (https://becarioprecario.bitbucket.io/inla-gitbook/ch-survival.html#se
> > mi-parametric-estimation-cox-proportional-hazards) below.  With no
> > fixed effect prior, the model reports mean = -0.047 and sd = 0.179 for
> > trt. Changing the mean between 1, -1, 10, and -10 with a constant
> > precision (of 10 for example) on the prior resulted in consistent
> > estimates for trt. Increasing the precision would increase the
> > estimate closer to 0, and decrease the standard deviation. Am I
> > missing something here? Thanks so much.
> >
> > veteran$time.m <- round(veteran$time / 30, 3)
> > sinla.vet <- inla.surv(veteran$time.m, veteran$status)
> >
> > # No trt prior
> > coxinla.vet <- inla(sinla.vet ~  1 + trt, data = veteran,
> >                     family = "coxph",
> >                     control.hazard = list(hyper = list(prec =
> > list(param = c(0.001, 0.001)))))
> > summary(coxinla.vet) # beta <- -0.047 (+/-0.179)
> >
> > # Prior on trt - change mean and precision as needed
> > coxinla.vet2 <- inla(sinla.vet ~ 1   + trt, data = veteran,
> >                     family = "coxph",
> >                     control.hazard = list(hyper = list(prec =
> > list(param = c(0.001, 0.001)))),
> >                     control.fixed = list(mean = list(trt = 1), prec =
> > list(trt=10)))
> > summary(coxinla.vet2)
> >
